## 9. Security affecting the endpoint

Method-level: `addOwner` is annotated `@PreAuthorize("hasRole(@roles.OWNER_ADMIN)")` (controller line 115-117). `@roles` resolves to the `Roles` bean.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/security/Roles.java`:

```java
@Component
public class Roles {
    public final String OWNER_ADMIN = "ROLE_OWNER_ADMIN";
    public final String VET_ADMIN = "ROLE_VET_ADMIN";
    public final String ADMIN = "ROLE_ADMIN";
}
```

So creating an owner requires role `ROLE_OWNER_ADMIN` — but only when security is enabled.

Two mutually exclusive configs gated by the `petclinic.security.enable` property:

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/security/BasicAuthenticationConfig.java` — active when `petclinic.security.enable=true`. Enables HTTP Basic, `@EnableMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)` (so `@PreAuthorize` is enforced), JDBC-backed users/roles, CSRF disabled, and a `BCryptPasswordEncoder` bean.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/security/DisableSecurityConfig.java` — active when `petclinic.security.enable=false`. `anyRequest().permitAll()`, CSRF disabled.

In `application.properties` (line 49) `petclinic.security.enable=false`, so by default security is OFF: no authentication required, method security is not enabled, and the `@PreAuthorize` on `addOwner` is effectively not enforced. There is no `PasswordEncoder`/password handling on the owner-create path itself (BCrypt only exists for user login when security is enabled).

